## Iteration 14 — 2026-04-16

Two parked items shipped; both driven by concrete friction observed
in the dial menu / Ring of Pain pair from iter-13. Visual verification
still blocked (fifth iteration).

### What shipped

* **``Anchor.TOP_CENTER`` / ``BOTTOM_CENTER`` / ``LEFT_CENTER`` /
  ``RIGHT_CENTER``** as enum aliases. Python enum's duplicate-value
  semantics: ``Anchor.TOP_CENTER is Anchor.TOP`` == ``True``. The
  existing ``TOP`` / ``BOTTOM`` anchors already centre on the
  perpendicular axis, but naming them ``*_CENTER`` at the call site
  reads more clearly when the author wants to signal *"centred at
  top"* rather than just *"top"*.
* **``saga2d.Selector[T]``** — choose-one-of-N state helper. Wraps
  the ``(i ± 1) % N`` pattern that both Ring of Pain and the dial
  menu had reinvented in iter-13. Features: ``value`` / ``index``
  /  ``next()`` / ``prev()`` / ``set_index()`` / ``replace()``
  (with ``keep_index`` flag for roguelike floor-descent cases).
  12 property tests. Dial menu migrated; Ring of Pain kept its
  manual ``player_idx`` because its "selector" mutates the node
  list in-place per interaction — a structurally different pattern.

609 mock-backend tests pass.

### Why Selector, why now

iter-13 shipped the second example (dial menu) specifically as a
generalisation check. The check exposed a direct duplication:
both games wrote ``(self.selected + 1) % len(self.options)``
style arithmetic. A third game would inevitably do the same.
Selector closes the loop:

    # dial menu before iter-14
    self.options = ["New Game", ...]
    self.selected = 0
    def rotate_cw(self):
        self.selected = (self.selected + 1) % len(self.options)

    # dial menu after iter-14
    self.selector = Selector(["New Game", ...])
    def rotate_cw(self):
        self.selector.next()

And the reactive-HUD pattern threads through cleanly:

    Label(lambda: self.selector.value, text_style="heading")

### Meta: five iterations unrendered

Pyglet subprocess display still returns empty ``screens``. This is
now five iterations (10, 11, 12, 13, 14) without a visual
cross-check. The mock-backend test count has climbed from 566 at
iter-9's last rendered state to 609 now — everything that can be
covered by mocks is being covered.

Worth noting the pattern: every iteration since the display broke
has shipped *pure API / refactor* work. That's not a coincidence
— without visual feedback, the iterations optimised toward code
quality (introspectable controls, declarative ui, deduping
_lighten_color, factoring ReactiveValue, Selector) rather than
toward visual polish (bundled font, palette tweaks, layout
tuning). That's probably a healthy forcing function.

### API friction noticed this iteration

1. **``Selector`` is over-general** for dial-menu's needs. The
   menu doesn't need ``replace`` or ``keep_index`` semantics;
   those were added for the "third game might be a roguelike"
   case that hasn't arrived yet. Keeping them unused is fine —
   they're tested and documented — but flagged as speculative.
2. **Backward-compat shims on DialMenuScene (``options``,
   ``selected`` properties)** exist only to keep iter-13's seven
   tests passing. Per CLAUDE.md rule 6 ("don't deprecate, delete"),
   the tests should update to use ``scene.selector.value`` and the
   shims should go. Short-term convenience; flagged for
   iteration 15.
3. **Anchor enum aliases need to show up in serialisers**. If
   saga2d ever ships save-state that includes anchor names, the
   deserialiser needs to know ``TOP_CENTER`` and ``TOP`` are the
   same member. Not hit yet; flagged.

### Concrete steps taken this iteration

Two parked items (Anchor aliases + Selector) both directly
justified by iter-13's dial-menu evidence.

