tests, IDE integrations) can now consume scene introspection data
without string-parsing. Plus a clean refactor: ``summary()`` now
formats from the same JSON shape ``summary_json()`` exposes, so the
two can never drift.

### What shipped

* **``Scene.summary_json() -> dict``** — JSON-serialisable dict.
  Schema::

      {
        "scene": "DialMenuScene",
        "background_color": [16, 18, 28, 255],
        "controls": [
          {"keys": ["confirm", "space"], "method": "confirm"},
          {"keys": ["a", "left"], "method": "rotate_ccw"},
          ...
        ],
        "ui": {
          "type": "_UIRoot",
          "children": [
            {"type": "Label",
             "text": {"reactive": false, "value": "Dial Menu"},
             "text_style": "title",
             "anchor": "TOP_LEFT",
             "margin": 20},
            {"type": "Label",
             "text": {"reactive": true},
             "text_style": "heading",
             "anchor": "CENTER"},
            ...
          ]
        }
      }

  Round-trips through ``json.dumps`` so any consumer that speaks
  JSON can ingest it. Every field omitted when unset — a snapshot
  diff stays focused on actual deltas.
* **``summary()`` refactored to format from JSON**. The iter-20
  method is now a ~15-line formatter over the ``summary_json()``
  output. The two methods share the same data model, so any
  future extension (``include_bounds``, ``include_computed``,
  etc.) only has to add a field in one place.
* **12 new property tests**: JSON-serialisability round-trip,
  scene field present, background_color as list (not tuple, for
  JSON fidelity), background absent when unset, controls as list
  of key-groups, UI tree with nested children, reactive-flag on
  Label text, reactive-flag on ProgressBar value, text_style
  present when set and absent when not, anchor + margin shown,
  summary / summary_json never drift (consensus test), minimal
  dict on empty scene.
* **All 12 iter-20 ``summary()`` tests still pass** without
  modification — the refactor is byte-transparent.

757 mock-backend tests pass (12 new).

### The Keras parallel

iter-20 added ``Scene.summary()`` — Keras's ``Model.summary()``.
iter-22 adds ``Scene.summary_json()`` — Keras's ``Model.to_json()`` /
``Model.get_config()``. Same split:

* Human-readable form for debugging / bug reports / docs
* Machine-readable form for tooling / serialisation / snapshots

The two share one data source, same as Keras. This matters because
once a saga2d snapshot-testing library exists (iter-23 candidate),
it compares structured dicts rather than strings, which makes
diffs meaningful (``"moved TOP_LEFT → TOP_CENTER"`` instead of
``"line 5 changed"``).

