## Iteration 24 — 2026-04-17

Two parked items shipped together, both compounding directly on
iter-23: bulk-update env-var mode for the snapshot utility and
snapshot tests for the remaining two example games. All three
saga2d example scenes now have structured regression coverage.

### What shipped

* **``SAGA2D_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 pytest``** bulk-update mode.
  Recognises ``1`` / ``true`` / ``yes`` / ``on`` (case-
  insensitive). When set, every ``assert_scene_matches_snapshot``
  call overwrites its JSON file with the current value and
  passes, rather than raising. Standard ML-testing pattern for
  "I made a deliberate framework change that shifts many scenes;
  let me regenerate them all in one run and review the git diff."
* **Error message now names the env-var** so the developer
  learns about the escape hatch the first time a snapshot
  mismatches, not by stumbling on documentation later.
* **``tests/examples/test_ring_of_pain_snapshot.py``** +
  ``snapshots/ring_of_pain.json`` — covers the largest
  declarative scene in the examples (title + reactive HP bar +
  controls + 8-node layout fed by ``ring_positions`` /
  ``ring_budget``).
* **``tests/examples/test_tictactoe_snapshot.py``** +
  ``snapshots/tictactoe.json`` — covers the non-radial grid-based
  scene. Completes three-out-of-three regression coverage.
* **4 new env-var property tests**: overwrites on ``1``, accepts
  truthy variants, rejects falsy/empty/unset values, error
  message mentions the env-var for discoverability.

772 mock-backend tests pass (6 new this iteration; 4 env-var +
2 scene snapshots).

### Why the bulk-update mode matters beyond convenience

Without it, updating N snapshots requires N ``rm`` commands or
a one-off script. That friction discourages making intentional
framework changes because the post-change cleanup is tedious.
With the env-var mode:

    $ # before: delete each snapshot by hand, rerun pytest
    $ # after:
    $ SAGA2D_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1 pytest
    $ git diff tests/examples/snapshots/   # review
    $ git add -A && git commit -m "..."

The second workflow is the one ML teams use; ``jest
--updateSnapshot`` / ``cargo insta review`` are the same
pattern in JavaScript and Rust. Making framework changes cheap
to absorb is the point.

