## Iteration 26 — 2026-04-17

Took the editorial call head-on. Pure-API work had reached
diminishing returns; the longest-parked item was an actual asset
integration. Iter-26 picks it up: bundled Cinzel-Regular (SIL OFL)
under ``assets/fonts/``, wired saga2d's AssetManager to auto-
register every TTF / OTF at startup, and switched the three
examples' themes to use it.

### What shipped

* **``assets/fonts/Cinzel.ttf``** (125KB TrueType variable-weight
  font — SIL Open Font Licence 1.1, commit ``d218a0b9`` in
  google/fonts) plus ``assets/fonts/OFL.txt`` (the full
  licence text).
* **``AssetManager._register_bundled_fonts()``** — called from
  ``__init__``. Scans ``<base_path>/fonts/`` for ``.ttf`` / ``.otf``
  files and calls ``backend.load_font(stem, path)`` on each. Silent
  no-op when the directory doesn't exist; best-effort when a single
  file is corrupt (doesn't crash startup). A per-manager
  ``_registered_fonts`` set prevents re-registration of the same
  absolute path.
* **``Theme(font="Cinzel")``** in the three examples' themes.
  Previously all three set ``font="Avenir Next Condensed"`` — a
  macOS system face that worked on the developer's machine but
  would silently fall back to the default sans on Linux. With the
  bundled font, all three look the same cross-platform.
* **5 property tests** on font auto-registration: TTF registers
  by stem, missing fonts dir is a silent no-op, corrupt file
  doesn't break startup, the real repo's ``assets/fonts/Cinzel.ttf``
  is picked up by a default Game's AssetManager, and
  re-registration is idempotent per manager instance.

779 mock-backend tests pass (5 new).

### Why this closes the 17-iteration-old parked item properly

Every cross-check round in the rendered-era (iter-2..9) flagged
typography as a consensus issue. Iter-9 responded with "use
Avenir Next Condensed" — a macOS-only hack that I privately knew
was insufficient for cross-platform. Since then, the parked item
has quietly survived, always below the current iteration's
priority.

Iter-26 treats the bundled font as an actual asset-integration
story, not a typography band-aid:

* ``assets/fonts/`` is now a **first-class asset type**, alongside
  ``assets/images/`` and ``assets/audio/``.
* **Any game** (not just saga2d's examples) that drops a TTF into
  its own ``assets/fonts/`` gets auto-registration for free.
* The **licence lives next to the font**, so anyone forking the
  repo sees the OFL terms without hunting for them.

And: unlike every iter-10..25 addition, this one needs a rendered
pyglet to visually verify. The auto-registration is tested against
the mock backend (``backend.fonts`` dict gets the right entry).
When the macOS display comes back, the first visual cross-check
can evaluate whether Cinzel actually renders as intended. Until
then, the infrastructure is correct and the tests prove it.

