## Iteration 30 — 2026-04-17

Milestone iteration. Closed iter-26's micro-parked item
(silent-font-registration-failure → ``warnings.warn``) and paused
for a full-run retrospective at 30 iterations.

### What shipped

* **``AssetManager`` warns on font-registration failure**. Previously
  swallowed silently so a corrupt or unreadable TTF vanished without
  trace. Now emits a ``RuntimeWarning`` with the filename + the
  underlying exception. The silence-is-safe invariant (construction
  continues) still holds. Existing test updated to assert the
  warning category and message content, so future refactors can't
  re-introduce silence without breaking a test.

798 mock-backend tests pass (1 test converted from
silent-behaviour-check to warning-behaviour-check, no net change).

### 30-round retrospective — progress against "Keras for game dev"

The ``/loop`` started with a prompt to write a Ring of Pain-like
game using saga2d and reflect on what would make it "Keras for
game dev." Thirty rounds on, here's an honest accounting.

**What exists now that didn't at iter-0:**

Declarative APIs that form saga2d's Keras-analogue surface:

| saga2d construct                         | Keras analogue                |
|------------------------------------------|-------------------------------|
| ``class MyScene(Scene):`` + class-level attributes | ``keras.Model`` subclass      |
| ``controls = {(…): "method"}``           | ``model.compile(metrics=[…])``|
| ``Label(lambda: self.hp)``               | ``keras.Metric`` tracking live|
| ``ProgressBar(value=lambda:…)``          | training-progress callbacks   |
| ``Row(...)`` / ``Column(...)``           | layer composition helpers     |
| ``Theme(text_styles={...})``             | ``keras.config`` global state |
| ``ring_positions`` / ``ring_budget``     | layout utilities              |
| ``Selector[T]``                          | state helpers                 |
| ``Scene.summary()``                      | ``Model.summary()``           |
| ``Scene.summary_json()``                 | ``Model.to_json()``           |
| ``assert_scene_matches_snapshot``        | ML regression snapshots       |
| ``SAGA2D_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1``            | ``--update-snapshot`` patterns|
| ``game.save(slot)``/``game.load(slot)``  | ``model.save``/``load``       |
| ``Scene.walk()`` / ``find`` / ``find_all`` | ``Model.layers`` iteration  |
| ``Scene.summary(include_bounds=True)``   | layout-debug flag (no Keras analogue) |

Three example games (Ring of Pain, dial menu, tic-tac-toe) covering
radial, selector, and grid shapes — each under 200 lines, each
snapshot-regressed, each built entirely from the declarative stack.

Methodology: a dual-model cross-check (Gemini + GPT) with a neutral
synthesiser (Claude) that extracts consensus from drift. Documented
as a reusable utility in ``examples/ring_of_pain/ai_review.py``.

Docs: README rewritten around the declarative form, five-step
tutorial with CI-verified snippets, keras.dev as a per-round
build log.

**What's still imperative, and why that's probably fine:**

* ``Scene.draw()`` — still a loop over game-specific primitives.
  Every game has to compute its own circle/rect positions. A
  declarative alternative would have to understand game semantics;
  saga2d correctly declines.
* ``Scene._interact`` (and equivalents) — gameplay logic. Keras
  doesn't have a declarative analogue for "what does this layer
  actually do"; it just calls your forward pass.
* ``update(dt)`` — continuous simulation. Physics, AI, animation
  controllers. Genuinely imperative.

**What was parked and never picked up:**

Only one item from the rendered-era cross-check consensus made it
all the way through without being shipped: **visual verification**.
Pyglet-cocoa's empty-screens state has blocked subprocess rendering
since iter-10. Every iter-10..30 addition has been verified with
the mock backend. This has been the biggest non-ideal thing about
the run — no fresh cross-check round in 20 iterations — but it's
not something saga2d could fix.

**Meta: how "mock-only" changed the work.**

Compare the editorial character of iter-1..9 (rendered) vs
iter-10..30 (mock-only):

* iter-1..9 shipped API + iterated on *visual polish* (title font,
  label placement, palette).
* iter-10..30 shipped API + iterated on *introspection, testing,
  and documentation*.

Without visual feedback, iterations routed effort toward
debuggability. ``walk`` / ``summary`` / ``summary_json`` /
``assert_scene_matches_snapshot`` / ``include_bounds`` exist
because there was no easier thing to do. Accidentally, the
framework is now more inspectable at runtime than most rendered
peers.

**What's genuinely suboptimal at iter-30:**

1. **Cross-platform font fallback is still partial**. The bundled
   Cinzel TTF was iter-26's answer; iter-27 made family-name
   resolution correct. But pyglet's fallback chain when a font
   fails to load hasn't been exercised at all because rendering
   has been blocked. The infrastructure is right; the aesthetic
   outcome on non-macOS systems is untested.
2. **Sprite and audio subsystems remain formally untested through
   examples**. Save/load got exercised in iter-29; the other two
   remain. A fourth example game that uses all three subsystems
   would close the loop, but would need asset bundling.
3. **The declarative-for-everything dream stops at ``draw()``**.
   Three example games all drop into primitive drawing inside a
   procedural loop. A ``NodeRing(nodes=…)`` or ``GridBoard(cells=…)``
   higher-level primitive would keep the declarative surface
   unbroken, but would be game-pattern-specific in a way that's
   awkward to put in a generic framework.
4. **Real users are zero**. The saga2d code has been reviewed only
   by two LLMs (the cross-check loop). The README has been tested
   only by CI-verifying its sample. Every iteration has claimed
   APIs compose cleanly because the three example games compose
   cleanly; a fourth author might find cases where they don't.

**The editorial call at iter-30:**

The stack is close to done for the use cases it covered. Further
iterations would either:

(a) **Wait for pyglet to come back** and spend several rounds
    evaluating iter-10..30 cumulatively through the cross-check
    loop. Real aesthetic feedback, likely small visual fixes.

(b) **Ship a fourth example** exercising sprites + audio. Requires
    asset-bundling decisions; biggest remaining untested-subsystem
    coverage gap.

(c) **Declare the stack complete** for the ``/loop`` run and stop
    adding. The API is cohesive enough that further additions
    risk being over-fitting to edge cases.

All three are reasonable. The ``/loop`` fires again in ~5 minutes,
so the decision is the *next* iteration's.

