## Iteration 31 — 2026-04-17

Closed the "parity" parked item. iter-29 added save/load to Ring of
Pain; iter-31 brings dial_menu and tic-tac-toe into the same story.
All three examples now round-trip their state through saga2d's
SaveManager, and all three exercise the reactive-HUD-after-load
composition.

### What shipped

* **``DialMenuScene.get_save_state`` / ``load_save_state``** —
  persists ``selector.index``, the ``options`` list itself (so an
  old save survives a menu-item rename), and the status text.
* **``TicTacToeScene.get_save_state`` / ``load_save_state``** —
  persists the full board, cursor, whose turn, and the winner +
  winning_line (for a finished game).
* **6 round-trip tests** across two new test files:
    - dial_menu: preserves selection/status/options; reactive
      heading label shows the loaded option; save includes options
      list for version-drift safety.
    - tic-tac-toe: mid-match round-trip; post-winner round-trip
      preserves winner + winning_line; reactive status label
      (``self._status_text`` bound method) shows the loaded turn.

804 mock-backend tests pass (6 new).

### All three examples now share the save/load story

| Example        | iter added | Persisted state                              |
|---------------|-----------|----------------------------------------------|
| Ring of Pain  | iter-29   | player_idx, HP, coins, level, nodes list     |
| dial_menu     | iter-31   | selector.index, options, status              |
| tic-tac-toe   | iter-31   | board, cursor, turn, winner, winning_line    |

Every persisted field is JSON-serialisable and every example's
reactive HUD Labels pick up the loaded values on the next tick
without any post-load refresh — the iter-4 ``Label(lambda: …)``
pattern composes cleanly with iter-29's save path for every game.

### The "version drift" detail

``DialMenuScene.get_save_state`` includes the ``options`` list in
the save file, not just the selected index. Why: a future version
that adds a new menu item would shift indices. An old save that
read "selected index 3" would resolve to a different option
after the rename. Saving the options list lets
``load_save_state`` call ``selector.replace(...)`` with the
historical options, then ``set_index`` lands the player on the
option they chose.

This is the kind of save-format thinking that matters at save
file v1 — decisions made now survive across any future schema
migration. The test ``test_save_includes_options_for_version_drift``
asserts the pattern so it can't be regressed out.

### Meta: twenty-two iterations unrendered

| iter | shipped                                                  |
|------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| 10–30| (see earlier rows)                                      |
|  31  | Save/load parity across dial_menu + tic-tac-toe          |

Mock-test count 566 → 804. Save/load is now fully exercised
across all three example games. The remaining "untested
subsystems" from iter-25's retrospective are audio and sprites,
both of which would need asset bundling to demo.

### API friction noticed this iteration

1. **Three ``get_save_state`` / ``load_save_state`` pairs across
   three scenes have real structural repetition** (list fields,
   build dict, reverse). A ``@persist_state("player_idx", "hp",
   "coins", …)`` decorator on Scene subclasses would auto-generate
   the pair. Not urgent — three manual implementations are
   readable — but the fourth would be the moment to factor.
2. **Winner / winning_line None round-trip** in tic-tac-toe needed
   an explicit ``None`` check because the tuple field was stored
   as a list in JSON. ``load_save_state`` reads ``wl = state[…];
   self.winning_line = tuple(wl) if wl is not None else None``.
   This hand-written codec per nullable-tuple-field is fine at
   this scale, would be friction if saga2d had a schema layer.
3. **Reactive-label-refreshes-after-load** is now tested in all
   three games and provably works. The Label(lambda: …) pattern
   from iter-4 is more composable than I expected at the time —
   it works across save/load without any coordination, because
   Labels read state live.

### Concrete steps taken this iteration

Two scene save/load pairs, six round-trip tests, zero framework-
level changes. The story from iter-25's retrospective has one more
closed item: all three example games have save/load parity.

