## Iteration 33 — 2026-04-17

Closed the last iter-25-retrospective parked item: **sprites**.
Following iter-32's procedural-asset pattern, a single PIL-generated
PNG replaces Ring of Pain's three-``draw_circle`` player pip with a
real saga2d sprite. Stack-complete declaration follows.

### What shipped

* **``scripts/generate_sprites.py``** — PIL-based generator. PIL is
  already a transitive saga2d dependency (via the screenshot
  harness), so no new package needed. Produces
  ``assets/images/player_token.png`` — a 64×64 gold disc with a
  soft halo, a solid core, and a dark centre. 3 KB after optimise.
* **``Scene.draw_image(image_handle, x, y, w, h)``** — the
  scene-level helper that was conspicuously missing next to
  ``draw_rect`` / ``draw_circle`` / ``draw_text``. Its absence
  is why Ring of Pain had been drawing the pip as three circles
  rather than blitting an image. Now game code never needs to
  touch ``self.game._backend.*`` for primitive draws at all.
* **Ring of Pain uses the sprite**:
    - ``on_enter`` loads the handle once via
      ``self.game.assets.image("player_token")`` with a try/except
      fallback (stripped deploys without the PNG still run).
    - ``draw`` calls ``self.draw_image(handle, x, y, 48, 48)``
      centred on the radial pip position.
    - The fallback path (handle is None) still draws the old
      three-circle procedural pip, so removing the asset doesn't
      break the game — it just loses a polish layer.
* **6 sprite-integration tests**:
    - PNG is bundled in the repo
    - AssetManager loads it by name
    - Scene caches the handle in ``on_enter`` (not per-frame)
    - ``draw_image`` fires during the scene's draw tick and the
      blit references the cached handle
    - Token follows the player: rotating CW three times
      re-positions the image blit
    - Missing-asset fallback path leaves
      ``_player_token_handle`` at ``None`` and produces zero
      image blits (procedural pip takes over)

817 mock-backend tests pass (6 new).

### The Keras story at "stack complete"

Thirty-three iterations ago the prompt was "write Ring of Pain
in saga2d and reflect on what would make it Keras for game dev."
At iter-33 the three subsystems iter-25 flagged as "untested
through examples" are all demonstrated end-to-end:

| iter-25 untested | demo in | iter |
|------------------|---------|------|
| save / load      | all three examples | 29, 31 |
| audio            | Ring of Pain       | 32    |
| sprites          | Ring of Pain       | 33    |

Paired with the declarative stack (controls / reactive HUD /
Row / Column / themed text / Selector / layout helpers), the
introspection suite (walk / summary / summary_json / snapshot
testing with env-var bulk-update / pytest fixture / include_
bounds), and the three example games (radial, selector, grid),
saga2d now covers the "Keras for game dev" footprint the loop
set out to build:

* **Declarative scene definition** — classes, class-level dicts,
  reactive labels, transparent containers.
* **Centralised theming** — named text styles, bundled font
  with family-name-aware registration.
* **Introspection** — Scene.summary() + Scene.summary_json()
  mirror Model.summary() + Model.to_json().
* **Snapshot regression testing** — assert_scene_matches_snapshot
  with bulk-update env-var mirror ML snapshot workflows.
* **Save/load** — game.save(slot) / game.load(slot) mirror
  model.save / model.load, and the reactive HUD composes with
  load without any coordination.
* **Asset pipeline** — AssetManager auto-registers fonts, loads
  images and sounds; the repo's own generator scripts produce
  SFX and sprites from stdlib + PIL so no third-party asset
  licensing ever enters the story.
* **Testing story** — 817 mock-backend tests covering every
  feature added in the run.

Further iterations would produce polish but few genuinely new
primitives. The stack is close to done for the use cases the
``/loop`` prompt described.

### Meta: twenty-four iterations unrendered

| iter | shipped                                                  |
|------|---------------------------------------------------------|
| 10–32| (see earlier rows)                                      |
|  33  | ``Scene.draw_image`` + procedural PNG + sprite on RoP    |

Mock-test count 566 → 817. Twenty-four consecutive iterations
without a rendered pixel. Every iter-10..33 feature is exercised
through the mock backend; the aesthetic cross-check is the one
outstanding thing the run couldn't do.

### API friction noticed this iteration

1. **``Scene.draw_image``'s absence until iter-33** is a real
   instance of "we kept not noticing this" — the three other
   scene-level draw helpers (``draw_rect`` / ``draw_circle``
   / ``draw_text``) were added across iter-1..3, and nobody
   (including the cross-check loop) flagged the image analogue.
   Ring of Pain drew pips as circles for thirty iterations
   because there was no image helper inviting a rethink.
2. **PIL is a transitive dependency** via the screenshot harness.
   The sprite generator leans on this — ``try: from PIL import``
   raises ``SystemExit`` rather than degrading, because a saga2d
   install without PIL is already broken for other reasons.
   Worth formalising PIL as a direct dependency so the expectation
   is explicit.
3. **The fallback pip path** (``_player_token_handle is None``)
   exists because try/except AssetNotFoundError in ``on_enter``.
   Same pattern as iter-32's ``_play(sound)`` helper. A small
   mixin ``OptionalAsset[T]`` on Scene would factor both. Not
   needed for three call sites.

### Concrete steps taken this iteration

One PIL sprite generator, one 64×64 PNG, one new
``Scene.draw_image`` helper, one Ring-of-Pain integration, six
tests. The iter-25 retrospective's parked list is now fully
closed.

### The "stack complete" call

After iter-33 the saga2d stack genuinely covers what the prompt
asked for thirty-three rounds ago. Remaining items for future
``/loop`` fires (if any):

* **Render** when pyglet-cocoa recovers — twenty-four-iteration
  backlog of visual verification.
* **AudioManager warning on missing sound** — one-line
  improvement parallel to iter-30's font warning.
* **PIL as an explicit direct dependency** (currently
  transitive).
* **Second user** — real-world validation that the API reads as
  well to someone who didn't build it.

None of these are blockers for calling the stack complete. The
next ``/loop`` iteration can treat them as incremental polish or
move on.

