## Iteration 46 — 2026-04-17

Shipped two small parked-item fixes in one iteration —
``ParticleEmitter.follow()`` and ``aabb_overlap(strict=True)``.
Both surfaced from specific friction points in iter-44/45 and
both are 20-LOC additions. Dodge refactored to use
``follow()``, PNG snapshot byte-identical to iter-45 (pure
internal cleanup, caught by the iter-41 regression net).

### What shipped

* **``ParticleEmitter.follow(target, offset=(dx, dy))``**.
  Attaches the emitter to a sprite. Every :meth:`update`
  call syncs the emitter's position to ``(target.x + dx,
  target.y + dy)`` before spawning particles. When the
  target is removed, the follow silently detaches on the
  next tick — no stale reference retained, no exception.
  ``follow(None)`` clears an existing attachment.
  Finite-offset validation mirrors the existing
  finite-position validation on :attr:`position`. ~30 LOC
  on ``saga2d/rendering/particles.py``.
* **``aabb_overlap(a, b, strict=True)`` variant**. Default
  ``strict=False`` (saga2d's iter-44 convention: touching
  edges ≠ overlap) covers tile-based games. ``strict=True``
  uses ``<=`` so bullets tangent to a hitbox tag as hits on
  first contact — the shoot'em-up case iter-44 parked.
  4 LOC on ``saga2d/util/collision.py`` (one branch).
* **Dodge refactor**. Replaced the scene-level
  ``self._thruster.position = (...)`` per-tick boilerplate
  with a single ``self._thruster.follow(self._player,
  offset=(0, 4))`` call at emitter-construction time.
  4 lines removed; emitter owns its own tracking now.
* **9 new property tests** (7 follow + 2 strict):
  immediate-snap on follow, target-movement tracking,
  offset applied per tick, target removal auto-detaches,
  ``follow(None)`` clears attachment, non-finite offset
  rejected, continuous spawning uses synced position
  (particles spawn where the emitter currently is, not
  where it was constructed), touching-edges-as-overlap
  opt-in, strict mode still rejects disjoint rects.

1282 total tests pass (9 new: 7 follow + 2 strict).

### The Keras parallel this iteration

Keras's ``Layer.add_weight()`` / ``Layer.build()`` encodes
the "subsystem owns its infrastructure" posture:
when you add a ``Dense`` layer, you don't have to wire its
weight tensors to the optimizer yourself — the layer
registers them. saga2d's ParticleEmitter had a similar gap:
when you attached it to a sprite, *you* had to keep them
in sync every tick. iter-46 moved that bookkeeping into
the emitter, same Keras-shape as moving weight-registration
into the layer.

### Iteration pattern still holds

iter-45 parked "``emitter.follow`` would be a nicer API" ↓
iter-46 shipped it in 30 LOC + 7 tests, used it in dodge
without changing any rendered output. The parked-item →
framework-fix cycle is at 4 consecutive iterations (iter-
43/44/45/46 each closing the previous round's API debt).

### API friction noticed this iteration

1. **Sprite-to-sprite follow is not yet shipped**. iter-46
   only handles ``ParticleEmitter.follow(sprite)``. A
   common use case — an HP bar Sprite above an enemy
   Sprite — would want ``Sprite.follow(other_sprite)`` too.
   Not yet surfaced by any example; parked.
2. **``follow()`` doesn't offer rotation inheritance**.
   When a sprite rotation subsystem arrives (not yet),
   emitters attached to a rotating ship would want to
   rotate the ``offset`` with the ship's heading. saga2d
   has no sprite rotation yet, so this is hypothetical.
3. **No concept of "fire and detach"**. An emitter with
   a ``follow`` and a finite burst could usefully detach
   itself once the burst completes. Currently the caller
   must remember to call ``emitter.follow(None)`` during
   cleanup. Parked as minor API polish.

### Cross-check outcome

Skipped this iteration — PNG snapshot is byte-identical
to iter-45, the iter-41 regression net is sufficient
evidence that nothing visual changed. Saved ~30 seconds
of cross-check API calls for a pure refactor.

### Meta: 46 iterations, 6 examples, 4 clean diagnostic loops

| stretch        | what it produced                                     |
